footer{margin-top:28px;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;max-width:70ch}
@media (max-width:560px){ul.branch{padding-left:14px}ul.branch>li::before{left:-14px;width:12px}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){summary::before{transition:none}}
</style></head>
<body><main>
<header><h1>Industry Issue Trees</h1>
<p class="lead">Pick an industry. Each business unit opens into its three core levers, and each lever into three sub-elements that explain it. The badge on a lever says whether it mainly moves revenue or cost.</p>
<div class="legend"><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span> the lever mostly moves sales or income<span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span> it mostly moves spend or margin protection<span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span> it clearly moves both</div>
<div class="ctl"><div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Highlight levers"><button data-f="all" aria-pressed="true">All levers</button><button data-f="rev" aria-pressed="false">Revenue</button><button data-f="cost" aria-pressed="false">Cost</button><button data-f="bal" aria-pressed="false">Both</button></div><button id="ex">Expand all</button><button id="co">Collapse all</button></div>
</header>
<nav class="tabs" role="tablist"><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i1">🛒 Retail &amp; CPG</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i2">🏥 Healthcare &amp; Pharmaceuticals</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i3">🏦 Financial Services (Banking &amp; Insurance)</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i4">✈️ Airlines &amp; Travel</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i5">📱 Technology, Media &amp; Telecom (TMT)</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i6">🏭 Manufacturing &amp; Heavy Industry</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i7">🏛️ Government &amp; Public Sector</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i8">🛡️ Defense</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i9">⚡ Energy, Renewables &amp; Utilities</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i10">🤝 Social &amp; Non-Profit</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i11">🎓 Education</button><button class="tab" role="tab" data-t="i12">🤖 Data &amp; AI Projects (Cross-Industry)</button></nav>
<section class="ind" id="i1" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🛒 Retail &amp; CPG</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Buy it</span><span class="step">Move it</span><span class="step">Sell it</span><span class="step">Promote and price it</span><span class="step">Support it</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Merchandising &amp; Sourcing</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><p class="note">This BU mainly shapes cost and margin. The sale itself is earned in Store &amp; Channel Operations.</p><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Range and mix (what we sell)</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Assortment breadth and depth</b><span class="d">How many categories and options are ranged per customer segment. Too narrow loses shoppers, too wide ties up cash and space.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Margin and brand mix</b><span class="d">The share of high vs low margin items, and of third-party branded vs private-label products. Private label usually earns a higher gross margin.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>SKU rationalization</b><span class="d">Cutting slow-selling Stock Keeping Units to free shelf space, working capital and complexity.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Supplier commercial terms (what we pay)</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Rebates and allowances</b><span class="d">Volume rebates and marketing funds, typically booked against cost of goods, not as sales.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Payment days</b><span class="d">Longer terms release cash and improve working capital.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Negotiating leverage</b><span class="d">Scale, supplier consolidation and volume commitments that strengthen our position.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Sourcing and landed cost (how it arrives)</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Sourcing footprint</b><span class="d">Country and supplier choices that set base price, lead time and risk.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Freight and duties</b><span class="d">Inbound transport and tariffs. Landed cost, not invoice price, is the true product cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Private-label development</b><span class="d">Design, specification and quality of own-brand items, which set development cost and the margin they can earn.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we carrying the right range for each customer segment? Where do we lack negotiating leverage with suppliers?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Supply Chain &amp; Inventory</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Availability (right product, right place)</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Forecast accuracy</b><span class="d">How closely expected demand matches actual demand by product, store and week.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>In-stock rate</b><span class="d">Share of time products are on the shelf. Stock-outs lose sales and can send customers to competitors.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Replenishment and allocation</b><span class="d">Reorder frequency, safety stock and store-level allocation rules.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Stock efficiency (cash and margin tied up in stock)</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Inventory turnover</b><span class="d">How fast stock sells through. Slow turnover locks up cash and raises markdown risk.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Markdown and clearance</b><span class="d">How aged or excess stock is cleared and how much margin is given away.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Shrinkage control</b><span class="d">Theft, damage and spoilage losses, often concentrated in specific stores or categories.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Network and fulfilment cost (cost to serve)</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Distribution network design</b><span class="d">Number and location of DCs, which balance speed against cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Transport and last mile</b><span class="d">Mode, route and delivery cost per order.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Returns and reverse logistics</b><span class="d">Cost and speed of taking back and reselling returned goods.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we losing sales to stock-outs or losing margin to overstock? Is shrinkage concentrated in certain stores or categories?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Store &amp; Channel Operations</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 2 both</span></summary><div class="body"><p class="note">This is where most of the retailer&#x27;s revenue is earned.</p><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Traffic conversion</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Traffic quality and volume</b><span class="d">Footfall in store and visits online, and whether they match target customers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Conversion rate</b><span class="d">Share of visitors who buy. It grows sales without buying more traffic.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Service and store labour</b><span class="d">Staffing levels, skills and sales per labour hour, the largest store operating cost.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Space and network productivity</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Sales per square foot and same-store sales growth</b><span class="d">Whether each store earns a return on the space and rent it occupies.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Store network and format</b><span class="d">Where to open, refit, resize or close, the main capital decision.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Space monetization</b><span class="d">Concessions, subleasing and in-store media that earn from space beyond selling product.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Channel mix and economics</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Online vs in-store mix</b><span class="d">The balance that determines overall cost to serve.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fulfilment and returns cost</b><span class="d">Picking, delivery and returns cost, which decides if online orders are profitable.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Omnichannel effects</b><span class="d">Whether click-and-collect and online growth add to store sales or cannibalize them.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are underperforming stores a location, format or execution problem? Is online growth cannibalizing store sales or adding to them?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Pricing, Marketing &amp; Loyalty</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Price and promotion</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Price elasticity and positioning</b><span class="d">How demand responds to price and where we sit vs competitors.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Promotion effectiveness</b><span class="d">Whether promotions create incremental sales or subsidize purchases that would have happened anyway.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Trade spend and slotting (CPG)</b><span class="d">Funding and fees paid to retailers, which reduce how much of each sale is kept.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Spend per visit (basket size)</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cross-sell and add-ons</b><span class="d">Getting shoppers to add related items.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Premium trade-up</b><span class="d">Moving shoppers to higher-value versions of what they already buy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Bundling and pack-price design</b><span class="d">Multi-buys and pack sizes that lift order value.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Retention and marketing efficiency</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Loyalty repeat rate</b><span class="d">Share of customers who return, and profit from existing customers vs the cost of the programme.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Personalization and retail media</b><span class="d">Using customer data to target offers, and selling advertising to suppliers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Advertising return</b><span class="d">Whether awareness spend converts to traffic and sales.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are promotions driving new demand or just discounting sales we would have made anyway? Which customers drive the most profit?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Corporate Enablers (Finance, HR, IT)</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Finance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Working capital</b><span class="d">The inventory and payables cycle. Later payment and less stock release cash.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Overhead efficiency</b><span class="d">G&amp;A and head-office cost relative to business size.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capex discipline</b><span class="d">Choosing store and system investments by return.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>People</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Labour scheduling</b><span class="d">Matching staff to demand hour by hour to cut idle cost and avoid understaffed peaks.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Staff turnover</b><span class="d">Frontline attrition raises recruitment and training cost and weakens service.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Training and skills</b><span class="d">Capability of frontline and category teams.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Technology and data</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Data and analytics</b><span class="d">Quality of data behind forecasting, pricing and personalization.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Core platforms</b><span class="d">POS, e-commerce and supply chain systems that set what other BUs can do.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Resilience and security</b><span class="d">Uptime and protection of customer and payment data.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is high frontline turnover hurting service and cost? Do we have the data to personalize and forecast?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i2" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🏥 Healthcare &amp; Pharmaceuticals</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Discover</span><span class="step">Produce or deliver</span><span class="step">Get paid</span><span class="step">Reach patients</span><span class="step">Stay compliant</span><span class="step">Support</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">R&amp;D &amp; Pipeline (Innovation)</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><p class="note">A long-horizon investment whose output is the value of the assets it builds.</p><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Pipeline quality</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Pipeline size and phase mix</b><span class="d">Number of candidates and where they sit from discovery to Phase III.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Probability of success per phase</b><span class="d">Odds a candidate advances, which drives fund-or-stop decisions.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>R&amp;D productivity</b><span class="d">Cost per approved asset, reflecting trial design quality and talent.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Speed and protection</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Speed to filing</b><span class="d">Cycle time from discovery to submission. Faster filing means earlier launch.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Patent runway</b><span class="d">Years of exclusivity left at launch, which sets how long premium returns last.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Lifecycle management</b><span class="d">New indications, formulations and label expansions that extend an asset&#x27;s life.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Sourcing of innovation</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Build</b><span class="d">Develop in-house for control and full ownership of the value.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Partner or licence</b><span class="d">Share cost and risk, and access external science.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Acquire</b><span class="d">Buy assets or companies for speed, at a higher upfront price.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is our pipeline deep enough to replace revenue lost to patent expiry? Should we build, partner or acquire?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Manufacturing / Care Delivery &amp; Supply</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Production quality and yield (pharma)</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Batch yield</b><span class="d">Share of production passing quality release. Failed batches waste costly inputs.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Right-first-time quality</b><span class="d">Avoiding deviations, rework and release delays.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Plant capacity utilization</b><span class="d">Spreading fixed plant cost while keeping slack for disruption.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Care throughput (providers)</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Bed occupancy</b><span class="d">How fully beds are used, which drives capacity and cost per case.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Length of stay</b><span class="d">Time per admission, which affects throughput and cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Procedure throughput</b><span class="d">Cases per theatre, clinic or clinician, often limited by scheduling and turnaround.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Resource and supply reliability</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Clinical staffing</b><span class="d">Availability and mix of doctors and nurses, a common bottleneck and major cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Critical inputs</b><span class="d">Security of raw materials, APIs and medical supplies.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Supplier resilience</b><span class="d">Dual sourcing and buffer stock to avoid stoppages.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Where are capacity or staffing bottlenecks? How exposed are we to supply disruptions for critical inputs?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Market Access &amp; Reimbursement</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><p class="note">Determines how much of list price is actually collected.</p><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Coverage</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Formulary status</b><span class="d">Whether payers list the product, which gates the addressable market.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Reimbursement rates</b><span class="d">What payers agree to pay per drug, procedure or day.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Value evidence</b><span class="d">Health-economics data that justify price and support coverage.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Payer mix and contracts</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Payer mix</b><span class="d">Share of private, government and self-pay patients, each with different terms.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Rebates and gross-to-net</b><span class="d">Discounts negotiated with payers that decide realized price.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Contract structures</b><span class="d">Volume-based or outcomes-based agreements.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Revenue cycle integrity</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Claim denials</b><span class="d">Claims rejected by payers, often fixable through process.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Write-offs</b><span class="d">Billed amounts never collected.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Collection speed</b><span class="d">Days to get paid, which affects cash.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Which payers or contracts are eroding margin? Can we prove enough value to justify our price?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Commercial &amp; Patient/Physician Engagement</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">3 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Physician demand</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Prescribing share</b><span class="d">Portion of a physician&#x27;s relevant prescriptions going to our product.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Referral pipelines</b><span class="d">Patient flow from referring physicians and institutions.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Targeting and field effectiveness</b><span class="d">How well reps are pointed at high-value prescribers.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Patient demand</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Patient volume and acuity</b><span class="d">How many patients come and how complex their needs are.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Direct and digital engagement</b><span class="d">Direct-to-consumer marketing and digital channels that prompt patients to seek treatment.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Diagnosis and awareness</b><span class="d">Whether eligible patients know about and are tested for the condition.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Adherence</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Persistence on therapy</b><span class="d">How long patients stay on treatment.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Support programmes</b><span class="d">Nurse lines, reminders and financial help that keep patients on therapy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Refills and subscriptions</b><span class="d">Repeat volume driven by better adherence.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we reaching the right prescribers and patients? What limits adherence or referral flow?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Regulatory, Quality &amp; Compliance</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><p class="note">Protects the licence to operate. Its value is mostly avoided loss and enabled launches.</p><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Approval performance</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Approval speed</b><span class="d">Time from submission to approval. Delay shortens patent life.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>First-time success</b><span class="d">Filings that pass without rejection or major queries.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Regulatory strategy</b><span class="d">Choice of pathways and markets to sequence approvals.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Quality and safety</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Inspection and audit results</b><span class="d">Outcomes that can restrict or halt operations if poor.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Adverse-event and recall rates</b><span class="d">Safety signals and withdrawals with patient, reputational and cost impact.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Pharmacovigilance</b><span class="d">Systems for detecting, reporting and acting on safety issues.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Billing and legal compliance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Coding accuracy (providers)</b><span class="d">Correct coding that cuts denials and audit exposure.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Litigation and fines</b><span class="d">Product liability and regulatory penalties.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Data privacy</b><span class="d">Protection of patient information.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> What is the cost of one delay or rejection? Are quality systems robust enough to avoid recalls or sanctions?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">6</span><span class="bt">Corporate Enablers (Finance, HR, IT)</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Finance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Capital allocation across the pipeline</b><span class="d">Splitting funds between assets, phases and modalities.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cost of capital</b><span class="d">Funding cost for long, risky investments.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Working capital</b><span class="d">Cash tied in inventory and receivables.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>People</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Clinical talent retention</b><span class="d">Keeping scarce scientists, doctors and nurses.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Recruitment pipeline</b><span class="d">Attracting specialized skills in a tight market.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Skills and succession</b><span class="d">Building depth in critical roles.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Data and systems</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Electronic health records</b><span class="d">Quality of patient and trial data infrastructure.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Data usability for R&amp;D and outcomes</b><span class="d">Whether data supports research and outcomes-based contracts.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Interoperability and security</b><span class="d">Sharing data across systems while protecting it.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Can we attract and keep scarce talent? Is our data usable for R&amp;D and outcomes tracking?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i3" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🏦 Financial Services (Banking &amp; Insurance)</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Win customers</span><span class="step">Price and underwrite</span><span class="step">Manage risk</span><span class="step">Serve</span><span class="step">Fund and govern</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Distribution &amp; Customer Acquisition</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Acquisition</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Volume by channel</b><span class="d">New customers via branch, digital, broker and agent routes.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Application conversion</b><span class="d">Share of applications that become active accounts or policies. Drop-off is often process friction.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Acquisition cost per profitable customer</b><span class="d">Cost by channel, not just per sign-up.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Deepening and retention</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cross-sell ratio</b><span class="d">Products per customer, which raises lifetime value at low incremental cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Customer retention</b><span class="d">Retaining is usually cheaper than acquiring.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Share of wallet</b><span class="d">Becoming the primary provider of a customer&#x27;s financial needs.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Channel structure and dependence</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Owned networks</b><span class="d">Branches and tied agents, with high fixed cost but more control.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Intermediaries</b><span class="d">Broker commissions and their bargaining power.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Partnerships and embedded distribution</b><span class="d">Selling through other brands and platforms.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Which channels give the lowest cost of acquisition per profitable customer? Are we over-reliant on any one distributor?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Product, Pricing &amp; Underwriting</span><span class="badge bal">BU is even</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Pricing</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Margin and premium rates</b><span class="d">Net interest margin for banks, premium rates for insurers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fee schedules</b><span class="d">Account, transaction and advisory fees.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Risk-based pricing</b><span class="d">Setting price by customer risk and cost of funds.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Risk selection</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Underwriting criteria</b><span class="d">Who is accepted, declined or referred.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Loss ratio</b><span class="d">Claims relative to premiums earned, showing whether selection works.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Segment targeting</b><span class="d">Focusing on segments with the best risk-adjusted return.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Product portfolio</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Mix profitability</b><span class="d">Which products earn returns after risk and capital cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Product development and analytics</b><span class="d">Actuarial and pricing capability.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Speed to launch</b><span class="d">Time to bring a new product to market.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we priced correctly for the risk we take? Which products make money after risk and capital costs?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Risk Management</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 3 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Credit risk</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Default rates</b><span class="d">Loans that stop being repaid.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Delinquency</b><span class="d">Early arrears, an early-warning signal.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Collections and recoveries</b><span class="d">How much is recovered after default.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Insurance risk</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Claims frequency</b><span class="d">How often claims occur.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Claims severity</b><span class="d">Average cost per claim.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fraud control</b><span class="d">Detecting false applications and claims.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Portfolio protection</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Concentration</b><span class="d">Exposure to one sector, region or counterparty.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Reinsurance and hedging</b><span class="d">Transferring risk, which lowers volatility at a premium cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Stress testing</b><span class="d">How the book behaves in a downturn or catastrophe.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Where are losses concentrated? How would the book behave in a downturn or catastrophe?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Operations, Servicing &amp; Technology</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Process efficiency</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cost-to-income ratio</b><span class="d">Operating cost relative to income.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Straight-through processing</b><span class="d">Share of transactions or claims handled without manual touch.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Automation</b><span class="d">Removing manual steps to cut cost and error.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Customer service</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Digital adoption</b><span class="d">Share of customers served online, usually cheaper and faster.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Service and claims turnaround</b><span class="d">Speed of resolving requests, which drives satisfaction and retention.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Complaint handling</b><span class="d">Resolution quality and regulatory exposure.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Technology foundation</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Legacy systems</b><span class="d">Old core banking and policy platforms that slow launches.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cybersecurity</b><span class="d">Protection against attacks and fraud.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Operational resilience</b><span class="d">Ability to withstand outages.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> How much manual work can be automated? Do legacy systems limit product launches or resilience?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Funding, Capital &amp; Compliance</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 2 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Funding and liquidity</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cost of funds and deposit mix</b><span class="d">How cheaply and how stably we are funded.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Liquidity position</b><span class="d">Ability to meet obligations when needed.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Interest-rate exposure</b><span class="d">Sensitivity of assets and liabilities to rate moves.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Capital</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Capital adequacy</b><span class="d">Ratios against regulatory minimums.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capital allocation</b><span class="d">Directing capital to the best risk-adjusted returns.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capital optimization</b><span class="d">Improving capital use through structure and risk transfer.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Investment and compliance</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Investment portfolio yield (insurers)</b><span class="d">Returns on premiums held before claims are paid.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>AML and conduct compliance</b><span class="d">Anti-money-laundering and consumer rules.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Regulatory change</b><span class="d">Adapting to new requirements, with fines and remediation as the downside.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is capital deployed where returns are highest? How exposed are we to interest-rate moves or regulatory changes?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i4" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>✈️ Airlines &amp; Travel</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Plan capacity</span><span class="step">Fill it profitably</span><span class="step">Fly it</span><span class="step">Maintain and turn it</span><span class="step">Fund and staff it</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Network &amp; Fleet Planning</span><span class="badge bal">BU is even</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Network</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Route mix and frequency</b><span class="d">Which markets to serve and how often.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Hub and alliance strategy</b><span class="d">Codeshares and partners that extend reach and feed traffic.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Airport access</b><span class="d">Slots, landing fees and terminal access, which limit growth at constrained airports.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Fleet fit</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Aircraft type and gauge</b><span class="d">Matching size and range to demand on each route.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fleet utilization</b><span class="d">Hours flown per aircraft per day, spreading ownership cost over more flying.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fleet commonality</b><span class="d">Fewer types lower training, spares and maintenance cost.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Fleet financing</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Own vs lease</b><span class="d">Trades capital cost against flexibility.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Renewal timing</b><span class="d">Balancing new-aircraft fuel efficiency against capex.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Residual value risk</b><span class="d">Exposure to used-aircraft values.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Which routes destroy value? Is the fleet the right size and type for the network?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Commercial &amp; Revenue Management</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">3 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Volume</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Load factor</b><span class="d">Share of seats filled. Empty seats have no value once the flight departs.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Demand stimulation</b><span class="d">Marketing and offers that fill off-peak flights.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capacity discipline</b><span class="d">Not adding seats faster than demand.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Price</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Yield</b><span class="d">Fare earned per passenger-mile.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fare-class mix</b><span class="d">Balance of business and leisure, premium and economy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Dynamic pricing</b><span class="d">Adjusting fares to demand and booking timing.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Non-ticket revenue and channels</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Ancillary revenue</b><span class="d">Bags, seats, upgrades, Wi-Fi and onboard sales.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Loyalty programme</b><span class="d">Co-brand cards and miles sold to partners, plus the liability to honour miles.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Distribution mix</b><span class="d">Direct vs GDS and agents, with different fees for each.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we filling seats by discounting too heavily? How much can ancillaries and loyalty add?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Flight Operations &amp; Crew</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Fuel</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Fuel efficiency</b><span class="d">Burn per seat-mile, driven by aircraft, routing and operating practice.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fuel hedging</b><span class="d">Locking prices to reduce exposure to swings.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Flight planning</b><span class="d">Route choice, which also affects navigation charges.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Crew</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Crew productivity</b><span class="d">Flying hours per crew member.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Scheduling and rostering</b><span class="d">How rosters are built, which drives overtime and disruption.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Availability and training</b><span class="d">Enough qualified crew to operate the schedule.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Reliability</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>On-time performance</b><span class="d">Share of flights on schedule, which shapes customer experience.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Disruption recovery</b><span class="d">How fast operations return to normal after delays.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Compensation exposure</b><span class="d">Cost of passenger rights claims.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> How exposed are we to fuel price swings? Is crew scheduling driving avoidable overtime or disruption?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Maintenance &amp; Ground Operations</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Aircraft health</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Aircraft availability</b><span class="d">Share of the fleet ready to fly. Grounded aircraft earn nothing.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Planned vs unscheduled maintenance</b><span class="d">Well-planned checks avoid costly groundings.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Spare parts availability</b><span class="d">Right parts in the right place, balanced against inventory cost.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Ground performance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Turnaround time</b><span class="d">Faster turns allow more flying per day.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Baggage handling</b><span class="d">Reliability of delivery, a leading source of complaints.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Handling quality</b><span class="d">Consistency of check-in, boarding and cleaning.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Sourcing model</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>In-source or outsource MRO</b><span class="d">Control and capability vs cost and flexibility.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Third-party services</b><span class="d">Selling maintenance or handling to other airlines.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Vendor management</b><span class="d">Contract performance and service levels.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is unscheduled maintenance grounding planes? Should we in-source or outsource ground services?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Corporate: Finance, Labour Relations &amp; IT</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Finance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Balance sheet strength</b><span class="d">Debt load and resilience to demand shocks.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cost of capital</b><span class="d">Funding cost for aircraft.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Liquidity buffers</b><span class="d">Cash and credit lines to ride out downturns.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>People and compliance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Union agreements</b><span class="d">Terms that shape cost and change ability.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Labour flexibility</b><span class="d">How quickly capacity and crew can be adjusted.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Safety and regulatory compliance</b><span class="d">Non-negotiable licence-to-operate requirements.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Technology</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Reservation systems</b><span class="d">Core systems for selling and revenue management.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Operations systems</b><span class="d">Scheduling, crew and disruption tools.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cybersecurity</b><span class="d">Protecting systems and passenger data.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Can we survive a demand shock with our debt load? How rigid are labour contracts when we need to change capacity?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i5" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>📱 Technology, Media &amp; Telecom (TMT)</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Build it</span><span class="step">Acquire users</span><span class="step">Earn from them</span><span class="step">Run it</span><span class="step">Govern it</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Product, Content &amp; Engineering</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Delivery</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Feature and release velocity</b><span class="d">How fast valuable features ship.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Technical debt</b><span class="d">Shortcuts that slow future development and raise failure risk.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Engineering productivity</b><span class="d">Output per engineer and how R&amp;D budget is allocated.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Engagement</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Time spent and DAU/MAU</b><span class="d">Daily-to-monthly active users, a signal of stickiness.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Personalization</b><span class="d">Recommendations that raise relevance and usage.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Product experience</b><span class="d">Speed, ease of use and reliability from the user&#x27;s view.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Differentiation</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Content library quality (media)</b><span class="d">Depth and appeal, a main reason to subscribe.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Roadmap differentiation</b><span class="d">Doing something competitors cannot easily copy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Design and research</b><span class="d">Insight into unmet needs.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we building what users actually value? Is the content or product roadmap differentiated?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Growth: Customer Acquisition &amp; Retention</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Acquisition</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Customer acquisition cost (CAC)</b><span class="d">Cost to win a paying customer.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Free-to-paid conversion</b><span class="d">Share of free users who upgrade.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Referral and viral growth</b><span class="d">Users bringing in others at low cost.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Retention</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Churn rate</b><span class="d">Share leaving each period, which caps the value of every customer acquired.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Activation and onboarding</b><span class="d">How quickly new users reach value.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Customer success and retention offers</b><span class="d">Interventions that keep at-risk users.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Growth efficiency</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>CAC payback period</b><span class="d">Time to earn back acquisition spend.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Channel mix</b><span class="d">Paid, organic, partner and referral, each with different cost and quality.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Enterprise sales productivity</b><span class="d">Output per salesperson for B2B models.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Does the payback period on CAC justify growth spend? What drives users to cancel?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Monetization &amp; Pricing</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Pricing</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Tiers and packaging</b><span class="d">Bundling features to match willingness to pay.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>ARPU</b><span class="d">Revenue per customer.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Willingness to pay</b><span class="d">How much different segments value the product.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Customer value</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Lifetime value (LTV)</b><span class="d">Total value of a customer. LTV to CAC shows if the model is healthy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Upsell and cross-sell</b><span class="d">Moving customers to higher tiers or extra products.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Bundling</b><span class="d">Combining products to raise value and reduce churn.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Advertising and fees</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Ad load and CPM</b><span class="d">How many ads are shown and what advertisers pay per thousand views.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Payment and app-store fees</b><span class="d">Take rates that reduce net price.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Discounting</b><span class="d">Promotions that trade price for volume.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is our LTV to CAC ratio healthy? Which pricing model fits customers&#x27; willingness to pay?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Platform, Network &amp; Infrastructure Operations</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Performance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Uptime and latency</b><span class="d">Reliability and speed of the service.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Network coverage (telecom)</b><span class="d">Reach of the network.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capacity</b><span class="d">Ability to handle peak load without degradation.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Efficiency</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cloud efficiency</b><span class="d">Hosting and compute cost per unit of use.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cost to serve per user</b><span class="d">The key margin driver as usage scales.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capex deployment</b><span class="d">Where infrastructure investment earns the best return.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Trust and safety</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Content moderation</b><span class="d">Keeping the platform safe and brand-appropriate.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fraud and abuse prevention</b><span class="d">Protecting users and partners.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Transparency</b><span class="d">Clear rules and enforcement, which protect trust.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Can infrastructure scale without margins collapsing? Where is capex best deployed?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Corporate: Finance, Talent &amp; Legal/Regulatory</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 3 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Finance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cash runway</b><span class="d">How long funds last.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capital allocation</b><span class="d">Split between growth and profitability.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Overhead efficiency</b><span class="d">G&amp;A relative to scale.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Talent</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Engineering retention</b><span class="d">Keeping scarce talent.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Stock-based compensation</b><span class="d">Equity as a retention tool and a real cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Hiring pipeline</b><span class="d">Ability to recruit at pace.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Legal and regulatory</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>IP protection</b><span class="d">Defending patents, brand and technology.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Data privacy</b><span class="d">Compliance and user trust.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Antitrust and content regulation</b><span class="d">Exposure to competition and platform rules.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Can we fund growth until profitability? How exposed are we to antitrust, privacy or content regulation?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i6" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🏭 Manufacturing &amp; Heavy Industry</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Design it</span><span class="step">Buy inputs</span><span class="step">Make it</span><span class="step">Sell and ship it</span><span class="step">Support it</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Product Engineering &amp; R&amp;D</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">2 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Cost of design</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Design-to-cost</b><span class="d">Setting a target cost up front. Most product cost is locked in at design.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Product complexity</b><span class="d">Variants and unique parts that drive cost downstream.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Part commonality</b><span class="d">Reusing parts across products to lower purchasing, inventory and tooling cost.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Speed and quality</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Time to market</b><span class="d">Earlier launch captures demand and premium pricing.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Design quality</b><span class="d">Getting reliability right early to avoid rework and warranty cost.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Prototyping and testing</b><span class="d">Catching problems before production.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Differentiation and launch readiness</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Customer-valued features</b><span class="d">Features customers will pay for.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Tooling and dies</b><span class="d">Investment required to start production.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Certifications</b><span class="d">Approvals needed before selling.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we over-engineering products relative to what customers pay for? Can redesign cut cost without hurting quality?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Procurement &amp; Inbound Supply</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Supplier base</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Supplier concentration</b><span class="d">Dependence on few or single-source suppliers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Contract terms</b><span class="d">Price, length and flexibility of agreements.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Supplier quality</b><span class="d">Defects and delivery reliability of parts.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Price risk</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Commodity hedging</b><span class="d">Managing exposure to raw material swings.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Price pass-through</b><span class="d">Recovering cost increases through customer pricing.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Index-linked contracts</b><span class="d">Tying prices to market indices to share risk.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Sourcing decisions</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Make vs buy</b><span class="d">What to produce internally vs purchase.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Inbound inventory</b><span class="d">Materials held, balancing security against cash tied up.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Inbound logistics</b><span class="d">Freight and duties on incoming materials.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> How exposed are we to input price swings or a single-source supplier? Can we pass cost increases on to customers?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Production Operations</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Volume</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Capacity utilization</b><span class="d">Share of installed capacity used.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Fixed-cost absorption</b><span class="d">Higher volumes spread plant cost over more units.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Shift and demand loading</b><span class="d">Matching production plans to orders.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Quality</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Yield and defect rate</b><span class="d">Share of output that is sellable.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>First-pass quality</b><span class="d">Output that needs no rework.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Scrap and warranty cost</b><span class="d">The cost of poor quality.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Speed and efficiency</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>OEE</b><span class="d">Combined measure of equipment availability, speed and quality.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cycle and changeover time</b><span class="d">Time to make a unit and switch products.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Energy efficiency</b><span class="d">Energy per unit, important in heavy industry.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Where is the bottleneck? Are we running enough volume to cover fixed costs?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Sales, Distribution &amp; Aftermarket</span><span class="badge rev">BU leans revenue</span><span class="sm">3 mainly revenue, 0 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Demand</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Order backlog</b><span class="d">Confirmed future orders, giving visibility.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Customer concentration</b><span class="d">Dependence on a few large buyers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Key account management</b><span class="d">Managing the highest-value relationships.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Price and mix</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Pricing power</b><span class="d">Ability to hold price.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Product mix</b><span class="d">Share of higher-margin products.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Discount discipline</b><span class="d">Controlling concessions in negotiations.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Service and delivery</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Aftermarket and service</b><span class="d">Spare parts, maintenance contracts and upgrades, often steadier and higher margin.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>On-time delivery</b><span class="d">A key factor in winning and keeping customers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Channel economics</b><span class="d">Distributor margins and outbound logistics.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Who are the most profitable customers and channels? Can aftermarket service smooth cyclical demand?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Corporate: Finance, HR, IT &amp; EHS</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Finance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Capex allocation and payback</b><span class="d">Choosing investments by return.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Demand-cycle timing</b><span class="d">Aligning capex to the cycle.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Working capital</b><span class="d">Cash tied in inventory and receivables.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>People, safety and environment</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Workforce skills</b><span class="d">Availability of skilled labour, often a constraint.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Safety record</b><span class="d">Incident rates, important for people, cost and licence to operate.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Environmental compliance</b><span class="d">Meeting emissions and regulatory standards.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Technology</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Plant digitization</b><span class="d">Data used to raise efficiency.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Industrial automation</b><span class="d">Robotics and control systems that cut labour and variability.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Smart products and data services</b><span class="d">Connected offerings that extend the business.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are capex plans aligned to the demand cycle? Is skilled labour a constraint?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i7" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🏛️ Government &amp; Public Sector</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Set policy</span><span class="step">Fund it</span><span class="step">Deliver services</span><span class="step">Regulate and enforce</span><span class="step">Support</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Policy, Planning &amp; Strategy</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Prioritization</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Policy priorities and outcome targets</b><span class="d">Which problems to address and what success looks like.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Program design</b><span class="d">How a program works, which drives cost and reach.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Eligibility rules</b><span class="d">Who qualifies, which decides scale and fairness.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Evidence</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Data-driven evaluation</b><span class="d">Judging what works using evidence.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Pilot-then-scale</b><span class="d">Testing small before national commitment.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Stop and redesign</b><span class="d">Ending or reshaping ineffective programs, which frees budget.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Stakeholder alignment</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Consultation</b><span class="d">Bringing in the public, agencies and experts.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Inter-agency coordination</b><span class="d">Avoiding overlap and gaps.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Implementability</b><span class="d">Testing whether policy can be delivered in practice.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are we funding programs that measurably improve outcomes? How do we decide what to stop?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Revenue, Budgeting &amp; Financial Management</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">1 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever rev"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Revenue</b></span><span class="badge rev">▲ Mainly revenue</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Tax base and rates</b><span class="d">What is taxed and at what level.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Collection and compliance rate</b><span class="d">Share of tax due that is actually collected.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>User fees and charges</b><span class="d">Pricing for public services and assets.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Spending discipline</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Budget allocation</b><span class="d">Distributing funds against priorities.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Procurement efficiency</b><span class="d">Value obtained on government purchasing.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Leakage control</b><span class="d">Losses from fraud, waste and improper payments.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Balance sheet</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Debt level</b><span class="d">Size of borrowing relative to the economy.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Borrowing cost</b><span class="d">Interest rates paid.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Debt service crowd-out</b><span class="d">Interest payments displacing services.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Where is the gap between potential and actual revenue? Is debt service crowding out services?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Public Service Delivery</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 2 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Access</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Coverage and reach</b><span class="d">Share of the eligible population served.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Wait times</b><span class="d">How long citizens queue, a visible measure.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Service turnaround</b><span class="d">Time to complete a request.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Productivity</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Cost per case or citizen served</b><span class="d">Unit cost of delivery.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Caseload per staff member</b><span class="d">Workload per frontline worker, balancing efficiency and quality.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Process redesign</b><span class="d">Simplifying steps and removing rework.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Quality and targeting</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Outcome measures</b><span class="d">Whether service improves lives.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Citizen satisfaction</b><span class="d">How users rate the service.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Benefit and subsidy targeting</b><span class="d">Whether transfers reach the intended recipients.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Where are the bottlenecks that delay citizens? Is cost per outcome rising faster than quality?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Regulation, Compliance &amp; Enforcement</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 1 mainly cost, 2 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Rule design</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Clarity</b><span class="d">Rules people can understand and follow.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Simplicity</b><span class="d">Fewer, clearer requirements.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Compliance burden</b><span class="d">Cost imposed on citizens and businesses.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Oversight</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Inspection coverage</b><span class="d">Share of regulated entities checked.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Licensing speed</b><span class="d">Time to grant permits, affecting business activity.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Risk-based targeting</b><span class="d">Focusing scarce inspectors on highest-risk areas.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Deterrence</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Enforcement consistency</b><span class="d">Applying rules evenly, building credibility.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Penalty effectiveness</b><span class="d">Whether sanctions actually deter breaches.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Appeals and adjudication</b><span class="d">Fair, timely resolution of disputes.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Are regulations achieving their purpose without excess burden? Is enforcement targeted at highest-risk areas?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">5</span><span class="bt">Corporate Enablers (HR, IT, Infrastructure)</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 3 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>People</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Recruitment and retention</b><span class="d">Hiring and keeping skilled public servants.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Workforce skills</b><span class="d">Capability to deliver modern services.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Hiring rules</b><span class="d">Processes that speed up or slow down recruitment.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Technology</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Digital service adoption</b><span class="d">Share of services used online.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>System interoperability</b><span class="d">Ability of agencies to share data.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Cybersecurity</b><span class="d">Protecting citizen data and systems.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Assets</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Infrastructure condition</b><span class="d">State of roads, buildings and networks.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Maintenance planning</b><span class="d">Preventing backlogs that become hidden liabilities.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capital project planning</b><span class="d">Choosing and delivering major projects.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Do legacy systems and hiring rules slow delivery? Are maintenance backlogs building hidden liabilities?</p></div></details>
</section><section class="ind" id="i8" role="tabpanel" hidden>
<h2>🛡️ Defense</h2>
<p class="logic"><span class="lbl">How the business flows</span><span class="chain"><span class="step">Define requirements</span><span class="step">Acquire capability</span><span class="step">Field and sustain it</span><span class="step">Operate and train</span><span class="step">Support</span></span></p>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">1</span><span class="bt">Strategy, Requirements &amp; Force Planning</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Threat alignment</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Threat assessment</b><span class="d">Understanding likely and severe threats.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Mission priorities</b><span class="d">Ranking what forces must be able to do.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Capability gap analysis</b><span class="d">Comparing what is needed to what exists.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Force structure</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Force size and mix</b><span class="d">Balance across land, sea, air, space and cyber.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Joint and allied interoperability</b><span class="d">Ability to operate with other services and allies.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Posture and basing</b><span class="d">Where forces are positioned.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Resourcing</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Budget alignment</b><span class="d">Matching funding to priority needs.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Alliance burden-sharing</b><span class="d">Partner contributions that ease national spending.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Invest vs sustain trade-offs</b><span class="d">Balancing new capability against upkeep of current forces.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is force structure matched to the most likely and most severe threats? Where are the capability gaps?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">2</span><span class="bt">Research, Development &amp; Acquisition</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 3 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Programme execution</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Schedule and cost performance</b><span class="d">Delivery on time and on budget.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Requirement stability</b><span class="d">Changing requirements are a major cause of overruns.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Milestone governance</b><span class="d">Stage-gate reviews that catch problems early.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Contracting</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Contract type</b><span class="d">Fixed-price vs cost-plus, which shifts risk between government and contractor.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Competition</b><span class="d">Number of credible bidders, which affects price and innovation.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Prime reliance</b><span class="d">Dependence on a few large contractors.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Technology</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Technology maturity</b><span class="d">Readiness before committing to production.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Innovation adoption speed</b><span class="d">How fast new technology reaches the field.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Prototyping</b><span class="d">Testing concepts early and cheaply.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Why do programmes run over budget or late? Are we over-reliant on a few prime contractors?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">3</span><span class="bt">Production, Supply Chain &amp; Industrial Base</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 2 mainly cost, 1 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Production performance</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Production rate and learning curve</b><span class="d">Output speed, with cost falling with experience.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Yield and quality</b><span class="d">Share of output meeting specification.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Tooling and facilities</b><span class="d">Capacity and readiness of production lines.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Supply resilience</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Supplier concentration</b><span class="d">Dependence on few suppliers.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Critical materials security</b><span class="d">Access to scarce inputs.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Sub-tier visibility</b><span class="d">Knowing the lower-tier suppliers behind key parts.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever bal"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Surge and industrial base</b></span><span class="badge bal">◆ Both</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Surge capacity</b><span class="d">Ability to ramp up in a crisis.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Dual-use capability</b><span class="d">Commercial work that keeps suppliers viable.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Workforce</b><span class="d">Skilled production labour.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Can we surge production in a crisis? Which single-source parts are a risk?</p></div></details>
<details class="bu" open><summary><span class="bn">4</span><span class="bt">Operations, Readiness &amp; Sustainment</span><span class="badge cost">BU leans cost</span><span class="sm">0 mainly revenue, 3 mainly cost, 0 both</span></summary><div class="body"><ul class="branch root"><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 1</i><b>Readiness</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Mission-capable rate</b><span class="d">Share of equipment ready for use.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Training quality</b><span class="d">Preparedness of personnel.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Personnel availability</b><span class="d">Enough trained people in the right roles.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 2</i><b>Sustainment</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Maintenance turnaround</b><span class="d">Speed and predictability of repair and overhaul.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Spare parts availability</b><span class="d">Parts on hand when needed.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Legacy vs new balance</b><span class="d">Share of budget spent on old systems vs new capability.</span></li></ul></li><li class="lever cost"><div class="node"><div class="lh"><span class="ln"><i class="lno">Lever 3</i><b>Deployment</b></span><span class="badge cost">▼ Mainly cost</span></div></div><ul class="branch"><li class="leaf"><b>Logistics reach</b><span class="d">Ability to support forces at distance.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Speed of movement</b><span class="d">Time to get forces and supplies where needed.</span></li><li class="leaf"><b>Forward basing and fuel</b><span class="d">Positioned support that extends operational range.</span></li></ul></li></ul><p class="q"><b>Critical questions</b> Is equipment ready when needed? What share of budget goes to sustaining old systems versus new capability?</p></div></details>
